schema: claim-evidence
object:
  title: Critical mass for a local currency is 20-30 active members
  type: claim-evidence
  claim: >-
    "A working local currency needs at least 20-30 active members who are
    genuinely buying and selling," and "launching without critical mass" —
    trying to launch to a whole neighbourhood on day one — is named as a
    common mistake; starting with a closest circle of collaborators is
    recommended instead.
  evidence: >-
    Stated as the article's own guidance, not attributed to an external
    study, dataset, or named case (the case studies section, e.g. Ithaca
    Hours' 1991 launch, is not linked back to this specific numeric
    threshold).
  source_lineage: src/content/docs/local-currency.md
  interpretation: >-
    A reasonable practitioner heuristic — matches general common-sense
    guidance for two-sided-market bootstrapping — but the specific 20-30
    number is asserted, not derived from a cited launch dataset across the
    named case studies (Ithaca Hours, Brixton Pound, Sardex, BerkShares).
  uncertainty: >-
    High — no citation for the specific number; treat as design guidance
    rather than an evidenced threshold.
  maturity: raw
  public_use: source-linked-unreviewed
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/local-currency.md
    transformation: quoted
    authorship: ai-assisted
